Fixed and committed (Fixes #1218). Before this, a -p run that failed still wrote its empty text over the --output file, so a script that re-runs yoyo -p ... -o answer.md lost its last good answer whenever the network, a rate limit or the key failed. Now a run that exits 1 doesn't touch the file at all, the way curl -o behaves.

Change
• What counts as failed: a new function in src/main.rs, run_failed_overall, decides it. A run fails if the caller already marked it failed (API error, a turn that died) or if the reply was a refusal or was cut off at an output limit nobody set. The JSON output's is_error already used this rule, and both now go through the one function. emit_output's own is_error parameter couldn't be used alone: at the two final call sites it misses refusals and default-limit cuts.
• On failure: the file is neither replaced nor created, and stderr prints a yellow line: <file> was not written: the run failed, so the existing file was left as it was (or ..., so no file was created).
• Unchanged: exit codes are the same, and successful runs still replace the file (#1214).
• Docs: one sentence added to docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md.

Evidence (both tables are on #1218: the before-table was posted before any edit, the after-table straight after re-probing the rebuilt binary)

| case | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| file existed, connection refused | exit 1, replaced, file emptied | exit 1, file unchanged, stderr says so |
| no file, connection refused | exit 1, empty file created | exit 1, no file |
| file existed, model refused | exit 1, file replaced | exit 1, file unchanged |
| file existed, run succeeded | exit 0, replaced | exit 0, replaced |

The refusal row was the same bug coming through the other half of the failure rule, so it's in this fix.

Tests
• tests/output_failed_run.rs runs the real binary against a closed port or a local stub, never a real vendor. It has the three failure rows plus a check that a successful run still replaces the file.
• Before the fix, the three failure rows failed and the success row passed.
• Recall control: I disabled the check with a NEUTERED marker (confirmed 1 marker before running), and the three failure rows went red. After restoring it (0 markers), all 4 passed.
• A unit test covers the two stderr messages.
• fmt and clippy (-D warnings) are clean, and cargo test passed 7636 tests with 0 failures.

Not fixed here, each on its own new issue since this push closes #1218
• #1219 (measured): --output-format stream-json -o file ignores -o entirely. It exits 0, creates no file and prints nothing.
• #1220 (read from code, not run): an interrupted run (exit 130/143) falls outside the exit-1 rule, so it probably still writes its partial answer over the file.

